@moduledoc """
Test data for <%= @app_module %>'s resources, made through their
actions: `generate(user())`, `generate_many(user(), 3)`,
`generate(user(name: "Ana"))`.

A generator is a function returning `changeset_generator/3`, which runs
the action when `generate/1` takes a value from it — the action's
changes, validations and policies included, so a record the domain would
refuse is never made. `seed_generator/2` writes straight to the data
layer and skips all of that: keep it for a state no action reaches.

Four rules keep them honest:

  * **Minimal and valid.** A default is what the action needs. A value
    the test asserts on is written in the test, as an override.
  * **Unique by `System.unique_integer/1`.** An identity is
    `System.unique_integer([:positive])`, not `sequence/3`, which is
    unique within one test only; two concurrent tests inserting the same
    value wait on each other's lock.
  * **Parents once.** `opts[:author_id] || once(:author, fn ->
    generate(user()).id end)` makes one per test, not one per record.
  * **Faker for what nobody asserts**, lazily:
    `StreamData.repeatedly(fn -> Faker.Person.name() end)`. The
    generators are StreamData, so they feed `ExUnitProperties` as they
    are.
"""
use Ash.Generator

# def user(opts \\ []) do
#   changeset_generator(<%= @app_module %>.Accounts.User, :create,
#     defaults: [
#       email: "user#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}@example.com",
#       name: StreamData.repeatedly(fn -> Faker.Person.name() end)
#     ],
#     overrides: opts
#   )
# end
